## DCTV

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/elias-4.jpg" alt="Street facade of the former Engine 31 firehouse on Lafayette Street, home of DCTV, with three red arched doors" /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/elias-5.jpg" alt="Interior of the DCTV firehouse set up for an event, with a lit dance floor, mirror balls and exposed brick walls" /%}

**Community TV Stations (public access?**

**More concrete descriptions of how they work,**

**What are the characteristics of the space? Who can use it? Who is the audience?**

**Describe the set/stage**

Started out as a series of workshops by filmmaking duo Jon Alpert and Keiko Tsuno-used cameras to help Chinatown citizens advocate in local issues. In 1978 relocated to its current facility, a former firehouse, aimed at educating the public in documentary production

In 2022 , DCTV opened New York City's first (documentary focused) nonprofit cinema.

The primary goal is to get the public interested in documentaries as a medium and documentary production, providing access to equipment most otherwise would not have. Facilities include a darkroom with lighting and a limited customizable stage, and offices with computers for editing and post-production. The studios are generally open to all, including anyone from the general public to experienced filmmakers, but expectedly they must undergo a period of orientation. The audience can literally be anyone.

Interior design of the space is heavily determined by the (stripped down) original fabric of the Gothic revival building, but small changes such as lighting, furniture and decorations give the spaces the hushed atmosphere of a movie theater. A palette of bright colors and metallic finishes gives the space an approachable and quirky industrial aesthetic.

## Spaces for Talk Shows: Ed Sullivan Theater

Built originally by playwright and dramatist Oscar Hammerstein as a broadway theater.

lost to foreclosure, fell through several hands, then in 1950 converted to CBS Broadcasting. The audience has shifted from in person to remote and global.

A theater in NYC which was, most recently, the location of the Late Show. A large auditorium for a live audience and a stage, with associated broadcasting equipment and programs. Auditorium designed to resemble a cathedral: domed ceiling with ribbed vaulting and stained glass windows, paying homage to the operatic background of the founder. Amenities include wardrobe areas, wings, fly-space, and more

The design of the late show set was geared towards creating an intimate space for conversation amidst the grand theater. The stage design echoes the grandiose architecture of the surrounding theater on a smaller scale, with precise molds used from the columns, and stained glass windows which change color. Artificially lit, the windows can change color based on the programming of the night.

