## BRIC TV

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/elias-9.jpg" alt="Corner facade of BRIC House, the renovated Strand Theatre in Brooklyn, with the BRIC and Media House signs lit at dusk" /%}

BRIC TV is a program produced by Brooklyn Information & Culture, an arts and media organization in Brooklyn, New York. Currently, The organization offers gallery space, radio and tv studios and a series of programs (the most famous of which is Celebrate Brooklyn!) BRIC TV brings in local creators and artists to create Emmy winning programs dedicated to reporting on local news, justice issues, and local culture. Some of their showings include comedy series, sci-fi animation, and a pizza documentary

BRIC originally started broadcasting radio from a series of rotating locations around NYC . The organization is currently located in the renovated Strand theater.

BRIC was founded as radio in 1979. The current facility, with its tv studio, came about in 2013. BRIC TV itself was founded in 2010

Different users have different levels of access to BRIC TV Space. BRIC offers intensives to familiarize the general population with tv production. Programs like this are generally open to Brooklyn residents. The Brooklyn Free Speech program allows users to have their own show on the network (they must however undergo an orientation).

To promote access to tv and video production. To promote community and interconnectedness. To report news and promote the arts.

How? BRIC is a registered non-profit organization. Their funding is a mix of public support, grants, and private donations.

Community access television is community created content typically narrowcast on local television networks→specific access for locals. Created from disenchantment with establishment broadcasting. Often grouped with Public Education and Government access programs (PEG).

1972-FCC issues Third Report and Order, mandating the top hundred cable tv systems to mandate public access television networks.

PEG struggles with balancing freedom of speech with open access. Sometimes creators are banned or discriminated against via being given less airtime. Hateful and adult content have also notoriously appeared at times leading to more controversy with this television model.

When well intentioned, PEG is grassroots television that works with the community from the ground up, rather than on executive orders handed down. Of course it has to comply with the cable network on which it's programmed, but with the move to employ internet technologies, that becomes less of an issue.

PEG has spread to Canada, New Zealand, United Kingdom, Africa, Australia, etc as a global phenomenon

George Stoney co-founded alternative media center at NYU to train the general population in creating television. His grassroots activism helped to shape regulations around community tv.

Sidney Dean: advocated with the FCC to reserve cable channels for ordinary television

Frank Massie: hosted surreal "Stairway to Stardom" show on public access tv, a surreal talent show

Stages for public access television tend not to be fixed the way they are for late night shows or sitcoms, because there are rotating hosts or shows. The space is customizable, the set changes all of the time.
